	<bitfield name="ColumnFlags" c:type="OmniColumnFlags" glib:type-name="OmniColumnFlags" glib:get-type="omni_column_flags_get_type">
		<doc xml:whitespace="preserve" filename="" line="0">A set of flags indicating properties of a detail view column.

The flags can be set by [property@Omni.ColumnBuilder:flags].

The unused bits are reserved for future use and must be cleared.</doc>
		<member name="pin_start" c:identifier="OMNI_COLUMN_FLAGS_PIN_START" value="1">
			<doc xml:whitespace="preserve" filename="" line="0">Pin the column to the left in the initial state.

Pinned columns are displayed in a separate area called a *frozen band*
(one exists for each direction).
The remaining columns are added to the *unfrozen band*.
The layout algorithm maximizes the area allocated to the frozen bands
while maintaining a sensible minimum width (hard-coded in the
application) for the unfrozen band.
Horizontal scrolling is per-band, so all bands are always visible at the
same time.

Pinning allows maintaining the visibility of key columns (like
identifiers) in a wide table.
The recommended approach when designing a table is:

- Pin the columns whose contents are short and fixed-width, such as
  scalar values.

- Leave one column containing contents of arbitrary lengths, such as log
  lines, unpinned and give it a very large width.

Pinning has no effect if the viewport can display all columns.

It is illegal to specify both `PIN_START` and `PIN_END`.</doc>
		</member>
		<member name="pin_end" c:identifier="OMNI_COLUMN_FLAGS_PIN_END" value="2">
			<doc xml:whitespace="preserve" filename="" line="0">Pin the column to the right in the initial state.

This flag is ignored for an aggregation detail view.</doc>
		</member>
	</bitfield>
