		<method name="eq" c:identifier="omni_transformer_eq">
			<doc xml:whitespace="preserve" filename="" line="0">Determine if the receiver is identical to `rhs`.

"Identical" here means producing identical outputs (including
serialized state data) for given inputs.

Depending on the transformer implementation, this may require locking
mutexes on both sides, which carries a risk of deadlock. The caller is
responsible for ensuring that deadlock cannot occur, regardless of the
order in which the mutexes are locked, as long as the order is constant
for each class.</doc>
			<return-value transfer-ownership="full">
				<type name="gboolean" c:type="gboolean"/>
			</return-value>
			<parameters>
				<instance-parameter name="self" transfer-ownership="none">
					<type name="Omni.Transformer" c:type="OmniTransformer*"/>
				</instance-parameter>
				<parameter name="rhs" transfer-ownership="none">
					<type name="Omni.Transformer" c:type="OmniTransformer*"/>
				</parameter>
			</parameters>
		</method>
		<virtual-method name="eq" invoker="eq">
			<doc xml:whitespace="preserve" filename="" line="0">Determine if the receiver is identical to `rhs`.

"Identical" here means producing identical outputs (including
serialized state data) for given inputs.

Depending on the transformer implementation, this may require locking
mutexes on both sides, which carries a risk of deadlock. The caller is
responsible for ensuring that deadlock cannot occur, regardless of the
order in which the mutexes are locked, as long as the order is constant
for each class.</doc>
			<return-value transfer-ownership="full">
				<type name="gboolean" c:type="gboolean"/>
			</return-value>
			<parameters>
				<instance-parameter name="self" transfer-ownership="none">
					<type name="Omni.Transformer" c:type="OmniTransformer*"/>
				</instance-parameter>
				<parameter name="rhs" transfer-ownership="none">
					<type name="Omni.Transformer" c:type="OmniTransformer*"/>
				</parameter>
			</parameters>
		</virtual-method>
		<method name="hash" c:identifier="omni_transformer_hash">
			<doc xml:whitespace="preserve" filename="" line="0">Hash the transformer.

The resulting hash digest will be used to equate a transformer to
another without needing access to subroutines specific to these
transformers.



The following invariant must be upheld:
`(hash_input(xf1) == hash_input(xf2)) == xf1.eq(xf2)`

where:
- `hash_input(xf)` represents all bytes fed to `hasher` by
  `xf.hash(hasher)`.
- `xf1` and `xf2` are two `Transformer` instances, possibly crossing
  a process or software version boundary.
  When this boundary is crossed, `xf1.eq(xf2)` means equivalence only in
  a logical sense because it is impossible to actually perform this
  method call.

&gt; This requirement makes it unsafe to feed the following values:
&gt;
&gt; - Memory addresses
&gt; - Hash digests generated through a standard mechanism provided by a
&gt;   programming language, such as Rust `Hash`, .NET `GetHashCode`
&gt;
&gt; These values are often non-deterministic across processes and runtime
&gt; versions. Furthermore, they have a relatively small entropy
&gt; (40 bits for memory address, 32 bits for `GetHashCode`), making hash
&gt; collision extremely likely.

&gt; A common pattern is to first feed a stable, unique 128-bit transformer
&gt; type ID to `hasher`.
&gt; This distinguishes different transformer implementations with otherwise
&gt; equal property values.
&gt;
&gt; The type ID is randomly generated, embedded in the source code, and
&gt; kept unchanged as long as the transformer's output behavior remains
&gt; fixed (transformers are modeled as pure functions).
&gt; It is regenerated whenever an implementation change can affect its
&gt; output or serialized state, even if its properties are unchanged.</doc>
			<return-value transfer-ownership="full">
				<type name="none" c:type="void"/>
			</return-value>
			<parameters>
				<instance-parameter name="self" transfer-ownership="none">
					<type name="Omni.Transformer" c:type="OmniTransformer*"/>
				</instance-parameter>
				<parameter name="hasher" transfer-ownership="none">
					<type name="Omni.Hasher" c:type="OmniHasher*"/>
				</parameter>
			</parameters>
		</method>
		<virtual-method name="hash" invoker="hash">
			<doc xml:whitespace="preserve" filename="" line="0">Hash the transformer.

The resulting hash digest will be used to equate a transformer to
another without needing access to subroutines specific to these
