		<method name="get_stream_start_time" c:identifier="omni_pipeline_snapshot_get_stream_start_time">
			<doc xml:whitespace="preserve" filename="" line="0">Get the minimum start time (`Tₛ`) of a given stream.

The *minimum start time* of a stream represents the lower bound of the
*start time* (`fTₛ`, the starting point of interval `fSTR`) in all
possible final states of the stream that it may reach from the current
state by materializing more portions of the stream.
If it is known that `Tₛ = fTₛ`, the stream is said to have its starting
point closed.

If the stream contains no materialized batches (i.e., `fSTR ∩ mSTR = ∅`),
this method will return `INT64_MIN`.
(An empty, complete stream has a stream time range
`INT64_MIN..INT64_MIN`, i.e., `fTₛ = fTₑ = MIN`.)

`stream_id` must be included in the pipeline snapshot.
    </doc>
			<return-value transfer-ownership="full">
				<type name="Omni.Time" c:type="OmniTime"/>
			</return-value>
			<parameters>
				<instance-parameter name="self" transfer-ownership="none">
					<type name="Omni.PipelineSnapshot" c:type="OmniPipelineSnapshot*"/>
				</instance-parameter>
				<parameter name="stream_id" transfer-ownership="none">
					<type name="Omni.PhysStreamId" c:type="OmniPhysStreamId*"/>
				</parameter>
			</parameters>
		</method>
		<virtual-method name="get_stream_start_time" invoker="get_stream_start_time">
			<doc xml:whitespace="preserve" filename="" line="0">Get the minimum start time (`Tₛ`) of a given stream.

The *minimum start time* of a stream represents the lower bound of the
*start time* (`fTₛ`, the starting point of interval `fSTR`) in all
possible final states of the stream that it may reach from the current
state by materializing more portions of the stream.
If it is known that `Tₛ = fTₛ`, the stream is said to have its starting
point closed.

If the stream contains no materialized batches (i.e., `fSTR ∩ mSTR = ∅`),
this method will return `INT64_MIN`.
(An empty, complete stream has a stream time range
`INT64_MIN..INT64_MIN`, i.e., `fTₛ = fTₑ = MIN`.)

`stream_id` must be included in the pipeline snapshot.
    </doc>
			<return-value transfer-ownership="full">
				<type name="Omni.Time" c:type="OmniTime"/>
			</return-value>
			<parameters>
				<instance-parameter name="self" transfer-ownership="none">
					<type name="Omni.PipelineSnapshot" c:type="OmniPipelineSnapshot*"/>
				</instance-parameter>
				<parameter name="stream_id" transfer-ownership="none">
					<type name="Omni.PhysStreamId" c:type="OmniPhysStreamId*"/>
				</parameter>
			</parameters>
		</virtual-method>
		<method name="get_stream_end_time" c:identifier="omni_pipeline_snapshot_get_stream_end_time">
			<doc xml:whitespace="preserve" filename="" line="0">Get the maximum end time (`Tₑ`) of a given stream.

The *maximum end time* of a stream represents the upper bound of the
*end time* (`fTₑ`, the ending point of interval `fSTR`) in all possible
final states of the stream that it may reach from the current state by
materializing more portions of the stream.
If it is known that `Tₑ = fTₑ`, the stream is said to have its ending
point closed.

If the stream has its ending point closed and contains no batches,
this method will return `INT64_MIN`.
(An empty, complete stream is assumed to have a stream time range
`INT64_MIN..INT64_MIN`, i.e., `fTₛ = fTₑ = MIN`.)

`stream_id` must be included in the pipeline snapshot.</doc>
			<return-value transfer-ownership="full">
				<type name="Omni.Time" c:type="OmniTime"/>
			</return-value>
			<parameters>
				<instance-parameter name="self" transfer-ownership="none">
					<type name="Omni.PipelineSnapshot" c:type="OmniPipelineSnapshot*"/>
				</instance-parameter>
				<parameter name="stream_id" transfer-ownership="none">
					<type name="Omni.PhysStreamId" c:type="OmniPhysStreamId*"/>
				</parameter>
			</parameters>
		</method>
		<virtual-method name="get_stream_end_time" invoker="get_stream_end_time">
			<doc xml:whitespace="preserve" filename="" line="0">Get the maximum end time (`Tₑ`) of a given stream.

The *maximum end time* of a stream represents the upper bound of the
*end time* (`fTₑ`, the ending point of interval `fSTR`) in all possible
final states of the stream that it may reach from the current state by
materializing more portions of the stream.
If it is known that `Tₑ = fTₑ`, the stream is said to have its ending
point closed.

If the stream has its ending point closed and contains no batches,
this method will return `INT64_MIN`.
(An empty, complete stream is assumed to have a stream time range
`INT64_MIN..INT64_MIN`, i.e., `fTₛ = fTₑ = MIN`.)

`stream_id` must be included in the pipeline snapshot.</doc>
			<return-value transfer-ownership="full">
				<type name="Omni.Time" c:type="OmniTime"/>
			</return-value>
			<parameters>
				<instance-parameter name="self" transfer-ownership="none">
					<type name="Omni.PipelineSnapshot" c:type="OmniPipelineSnapshot*"/>
				</instance-parameter>
				<parameter name="stream_id" transfer-ownership="none">
					<type name="Omni.PhysStreamId" c:type="OmniPhysStreamId*"/>
				</parameter>
			</parameters>
		</virtual-method>
		<method name="get_stream_next_time" c:identifier="omni_pipeline_snapshot_get_stream_next_time">
			<doc xml:whitespace="preserve" filename="" line="0">Find the next batch in a given stream (`NST`).

Given a scan starting point `scan_start`, The *next time* of a stream
`S`, `NST(S, scan_start)` is defined as follows:

- The maximum value of `t` such that range `scan_start..t` does not
  overlap with the currently materialized batch time ranges (`fSTR ∩ mSTR`).

- Or equivalently, the smallest member of `([t', INT64_MAX] ∩ fSTR ∩
  mSTR) ∪ {INT64_MAX}`

Here are some important cases:

- If `scan_start..t` is devoid of materialized batches, then `NST(S,
  scan_start) = TIME_MAX`.

- If `scan_start` is inside a materialized batch, then `NST(S,
  scan_start) = scan_start`.

`stream_id` must be included in the pipeline snapshot.</doc>
			<return-value transfer-ownership="full">
				<type name="Omni.Time" c:type="OmniTime"/>
			</return-value>
			<parameters>
				<instance-parameter name="self" transfer-ownership="none">
					<type name="Omni.PipelineSnapshot" c:type="OmniPipelineSnapshot*"/>
				</instance-parameter>
				<parameter name="stream_id" transfer-ownership="none">
					<type name="Omni.PhysStreamId" c:type="OmniPhysStreamId*"/>
				</parameter>
				<parameter name="scan_start" transfer-ownership="none">
					<type name="Omni.Time" c:type="OmniTime"/>
				</parameter>
			</parameters>
		</method>
		<virtual-method name="get_stream_next_time" invoker="get_stream_next_time">
			<doc xml:whitespace="preserve" filename="" line="0">Find the next batch in a given stream (`NST`).

Given a scan starting point `scan_start`, The *next time* of a stream
`S`, `NST(S, scan_start)` is defined as follows:

- The maximum value of `t` such that range `scan_start..t` does not
  overlap with the currently materialized batch time ranges (`fSTR ∩ mSTR`).

- Or equivalently, the smallest member of `([t', INT64_MAX] ∩ fSTR ∩
  mSTR) ∪ {INT64_MAX}`

Here are some important cases:

- If `scan_start..t` is devoid of materialized batches, then `NST(S,
  scan_start) = TIME_MAX`.

- If `scan_start` is inside a materialized batch, then `NST(S,
  scan_start) = scan_start`.

`stream_id` must be included in the pipeline snapshot.</doc>
			<return-value transfer-ownership="full">
				<type name="Omni.Time" c:type="OmniTime"/>
			</return-value>
			<parameters>
				<instance-parameter name="self" transfer-ownership="none">
					<type name="Omni.PipelineSnapshot" c:type="OmniPipelineSnapshot*"/>
				</instance-parameter>
				<parameter name="stream_id" transfer-ownership="none">
					<type name="Omni.PhysStreamId" c:type="OmniPhysStreamId*"/>
				</parameter>
				<parameter name="scan_start" transfer-ownership="none">
					<type name="Omni.Time" c:type="OmniTime"/>
				</parameter>
			</parameters>
		</virtual-method>
		<method name="get_stream_watermark_time" c:identifier="omni_pipeline_snapshot_get_stream_watermark_time">
			<doc xml:whitespace="preserve" filename="" line="0">Get the partial watermark time (`WT`) of a given stream.

Given a scan starting point `scan_start`, The *partial watermark time*
of a stream `S`, `WT(S, scan_start)`, is defined as follows:

- The maximum value of `t` such that range `scan_start..t` has been
  materialized (i.e., `scan_start..t ⊆ mSTR`).

- Or equivalently, the smallest member of `([t', INT64_MAX] ∖ mSTR) ∪
  {INT64_MAX}`.

For a complete stream (where the entire time range has reached the final
state), it is equal to `INT64_MAX`.

The stream must be included in the pipeline snapshot.</doc>
			<return-value transfer-ownership="full">
				<type name="Omni.Time" c:type="OmniTime"/>
			</return-value>
			<parameters>
				<instance-parameter name="self" transfer-ownership="none">
					<type name="Omni.PipelineSnapshot" c:type="OmniPipelineSnapshot*"/>
				</instance-parameter>
				<parameter name="stream_id" transfer-ownership="none">
					<type name="Omni.PhysStreamId" c:type="OmniPhysStreamId*"/>
				</parameter>
				<parameter name="scan_start" transfer-ownership="none">
					<type name="Omni.Time" c:type="OmniTime"/>
				</parameter>
			</parameters>
		</method>
		<virtual-method name="get_stream_watermark_time" invoker="get_stream_watermark_time">
			<doc xml:whitespace="preserve" filename="" line="0">Get the partial watermark time (`WT`) of a given stream.

Given a scan starting point `scan_start`, The *partial watermark time*
of a stream `S`, `WT(S, scan_start)`, is defined as follows:

- The maximum value of `t` such that range `scan_start..t` has been
  materialized (i.e., `scan_start..t ⊆ mSTR`).

- Or equivalently, the smallest member of `([t', INT64_MAX] ∖ mSTR) ∪
  {INT64_MAX}`.

For a complete stream (where the entire time range has reached the final
state), it is equal to `INT64_MAX`.

The stream must be included in the pipeline snapshot.</doc>
			<return-value transfer-ownership="full">
				<type name="Omni.Time" c:type="OmniTime"/>
			</return-value>
